  "direction_agreement": {
    "reference_direction": "ppo_point better",
    "conditions_where_ppo_uq_is_worse": 12,
    "conditions_total": 12,
    "any_condition_favouring_ppo_uq": 0,
    "reading": "`ppo_uq` is the worse arm in 12 of 12 sensitivity conditions. Direction, not effect size, is the quantity of interest: these cells ask whether the registered conclusion is an artefact of the synthetic OD model or of one reward scalarisation. No condition reverses it. Individual tests mostly do not reach the BH threshold, which is expected -- each uses five training seeds and ten evaluation seeds against ten and thirty in the main sweep -- so the consistent sign across conditions carries the evidence rather than any single test."
  },
  "weight_invariance": {
    "finding": "EXP-SENS-001 cannot test reward-weight sensitivity on the primary endpoint. Re-scoring a frozen policy under a different reward weighting changes no action: the policy does not observe the reward at evaluation time, so the trajectory, and therefore `left_behind_fraction` and `inductions`, are identical across all three weightings -- verified per seed, not merely on average. Only `total_reward` differs, and it is an arithmetic re-weighting of components already stored.",
    "consequence": "The 600 cells of EXP-SENS-001 re-simulated identical physical trajectories three times. The question the experiment was registered to ask -- does the method ranking move with the objective? -- is answerable only on the reward, which is how it is analysed here, and could have been computed arithmetically without simulation. Testing weight sensitivity on physical outcomes would require *retraining* under each weighting, which the design explicitly declined to do.",
    "verified_identical_across_weightings": [
      "left_behind_fraction",
      "inductions"
    ]
  },
  "caveats": [
    "Exploratory. These conditions were registered but the analysis is reported separately from the confirmatory families and generates no claim in the abstract.",
    "The OD variants re-score policies trained under the registered OD model, so they test robustness of the *evaluation*, not of training under a different OD world.",
    "The reward-weight variants likewise re-score w_balanced policies; a policy trained under w_cost might behave differently, and this design cannot say.",
    "Ten evaluation seeds and five training seeds per sensitivity cell, against thirty and ten in the main sweep, so these are less precise than the registered tests."
  ]
}